# Sanitized authentication snapshot v1

`import-sanitized-auth-snapshot.sh` accepts exactly this UTF-8 TSV format. It
is an **offline sanitized artifact**, not a database dump and not executable
SQL. The first row is the literal header below; every later row has exactly
eleven tab-separated fields in that order.

```text
id	username	password_bcrypt	display_name	role	status	group	quota	used_quota	request_count	auth_version
```

| Field | Contract |
| --- | --- |
| `id` | Positive decimal PostgreSQL user ID; it appears once in the separate allowlist. |
| `username` | Non-empty UTF-8 text without a tab, CR, LF, or NUL. |
| `password_bcrypt` | A complete bcrypt verifier (`$2a$`, `$2b$`, or `$2y$`); plaintext passwords are forbidden. |
| `display_name` | UTF-8 display text without a tab, CR, LF, or NUL. |
| `role`, `status`, `auth_version` | Decimal integers; `auth_version` is at least one. |
| `group` | Non-empty UTF-8 group name without a tab, CR, LF, or NUL. |
| `quota`, `used_quota`, `request_count` | Non-negative decimal integers. |

The allowlist is one positive decimal user ID per line, sorted strictly
ascending. The snapshot must contain exactly the same IDs, once each. The
SHA-256 companion is exactly one lowercase 64-hex digest plus newline (the
digest of the snapshot bytes). The signature is an OpenSSL SHA-256 detached
signature over those same snapshot bytes, verified with the operator PEM
public key.

No other user columns are permitted. An exporter omits email addresses,
API/PAT values, external provider IDs, Stripe customer IDs, remarks, settings,
inviter/affiliate data, 2FA/passkeys, OAuth bindings, sessions, tokens,
payments, logs, tasks, and every other business table. The importer writes
synthetic test-only values and never accepts SQL, a database dump, or a source
database URL.
